---
import H from "@/components/H.astro";
import Layout from "@/layouts/PageLayout.astro";
import Section from "@/components/Section.astro";
import List from "@/components/List.astro";
import Timeline from "@/components/Timeline.astro";
---

<Layout
    title="About"
    description="How Team V.A.N. is organized and where we work as a first-year FIRST Robotics Competition team serving southern Brevard and Indian River counties."
>
    <H>About Team V.A.N.</H>

    <p>
        Team V.A.N. (FRC 12148) is a student-led FIRST Robotics Competition team
        based in Palm Bay, serving students in southern Brevard County and
        Indian River County.
    </p>

    <Section aria-labelledby="organization-heading">
        <H id="organization-heading" level="h2">
            How the team is organized
        </H>
        <p>
            Team V.A.N. is student-run, with adult mentors coaching rather than
            directing. Four team leads manage organization and business,
            ensuring the team runs smoothly and that responsibilities are
            clearly defined.
        </p>
        <p>
            Beyond that, membership is not divided into fixed subteams. Students
            are free to work wherever they're most interested or most useful
            that week, whether that's wiring one day and CAD the next, and
            nobody is locked into one group for the season. Team leads
            coordinate the work and mentors provide technical guidance and
            safety oversight, but day-to-day problem solving is student-driven.
        </p>
        <p>
            Experienced students train new members and keep notes on both the
            technical work and how the team is run, so responsibilities and
            institutional knowledge can be handed off to future leaders as the
            team grows.
        </p>
    </Section>

    <Section aria-labelledby="timeline-heading">
        <H id="timeline-heading" level="h2">
            Our season at a glance
        </H>
        <p>
            Team V.A.N. runs on the standard FIRST Robotics Competition
            calendar, with a team kickoff of our own in November (exact date TBD) to get new
            members ready before the season starts. Exact dates shift from year
            to year; this is the shape of a typical season.
        </p>
        <Timeline
            phases={[
                {
                    period: "November",
                    title: "Team Kickoff",
                    description:
                        "New members meet, review safety basics, and get introduced to the team before the official FIRST season begins.",
                },
                {
                    period: "Early January",
                    title: "Kickoff",
                    description:
                        "FIRST reveals the new game. The team studies the rules, brainstorms strategy, and starts prototyping that same weekend.",
                },
                {
                    period: "January – February",
                    title: "Build season",
                    description:
                        "About six weeks to design, build, and program the competition robot, ending on Stop Build Day in late February, when the robot is bagged and no further changes are allowed until competition. The goal is to have it finished and competition-ready in time for our first event, the Orlando Regional, in March.",
                },
                {
                    period: "February – April",
                    title: "Competition events",
                    description:
                        "The team travels to regional events to compete, iterate on the robot between events, and work toward qualifying for the FIRST Championship.",
                },
                {
                    period: "April",
                    title: "FIRST Championship",
                    description:
                        "If the team qualifies, the season culminates at the FIRST Championship in Houston, Texas, alongside roughly 600 teams from around the world.",
                },
            ]}
        />
    </Section>

    <Section aria-labelledby="commitment-heading">
        <H id="commitment-heading" level="h2">
            Time commitment
        </H>
        <p>
            Outside of build season, members typically meet a couple of evenings
            a week, around 2 hours a session. During the six-week build season
            leading up to Stop Build Day in late February, that increases
            significantly, often including weekend sessions, as the team races
            to have the robot finished and ready to compete at the Orlando
            Regional in March. Exact hours vary by week and by how close an
            event is.
        </p>
    </Section>

    <Section aria-labelledby="access-heading">
        <H id="access-heading" level="h2">
            Who we serve
        </H>
        <p>
            Indian River County currently has no local FRC team, and students in
            southern Brevard often face transportation and financial barriers to
            existing programs. Palm Bay Magnet Senior High operates under a
            Title I schoolwide program, and Bayside High and Heritage High serve
            significant populations of economically disadvantaged students.
        </p>
    </Section>

    <Section aria-labelledby="facilities-heading">
        <H id="facilities-heading" level="h2">
            Where we work
        </H>
        <p>
            We meet and build in more than one place so the team is not
            dependent on a single partnership.
        </p>
        <List>
            <li>
                <strong>Mind Forge Guild</strong> is a local makerspace and our
                primary prototyping location, with woodworking and prototyping
                facilities. The makerspace has offered free access and weekend
                space to set up and practice on a field.
            </li>
            <li>
                <strong>Eastern Florida State College</strong> has pledged
                permanent space on campus for weekday meetings, planning, and
                team activities. We have access to their machine shop, 3D
                printer farm, tools, and storage. Metalworking faculty can
                assist with machining custom metal components, while we supply
                the raw materials.
            </li>
        </List>
    </Section>
</Layout>
